# JS control flow

## 1. Never use `continue` in for loops

Prefer nested `if` (or extract a helper) so the loop body reads top-to-bottom.

```js
// ❌ BAD
for (const row of rows) {
  if (kids.length < 2) continue;
  map[label] = value;
}

// ✅ GOOD
for (const row of rows) {
  if (kids.length >= 2) {
    map[label] = value;
  }
}
```

## 2. No one-line returns

`return` must be inside braces — never `if (x) return y`.

```js
// ❌ BAD
if (!root) return map;

// ✅ GOOD
if (!root) {
  return map;
}
```

## 3. Blank line before multi-statement returns

If a `return` is not a one-liner inside braces (`if (x) { return y; }`), put a blank line (or at least a space) before it.

```js
// ❌ BAD
function labelMap(root) {
  const map = {};
  if (root) {
    /* ... */
  }
  return map;
}

// ✅ GOOD
function labelMap(root) {
  const map = {};

  if (root) {
    /* ... */
  }

  return map;
}
```

## 4. Return values immediately — no temp `result` variable

When a branch already knows what to return, `return` it there. Do not assign into a temporary and return once at the bottom.

```js
// ❌ BAD — pointless temp variable
let result;

if (!location.hostname.includes('costar.com')) {
  result = {
    error: 'Open a CoStar property page in this tab, then scrape again.',
    currentUrl: location.href,
  };
} else {
  result = buildPayload();
}

return result;

// ✅ GOOD — return what you need right away
if (!location.hostname.includes('costar.com')) {
  return {
    error: 'Open a CoStar property page in this tab, then scrape again.',
    currentUrl: location.href,
  };
}

return buildPayload();
```

Bare void early exits (`return;` to skip work) are still wrong — wrap the work in `if` instead:

```js
// ❌ BAD
const add = (src, caption = null) => {
  if (!src) return;
  if (existing) {
    existing.caption = caption;
    return;
  }
  bySrc.set(src, { src, caption });
};

// ✅ GOOD
const add = (src, caption = null) => {
  if (src) {
    if (existing) {
      existing.caption = caption;
    } else {
      bySrc.set(src, { src, caption });
    }
  }
};
```

## 5. Max 4 levels of `if` nesting per function

Count nested `if` depth inside one function body. At 4 levels, extract helpers so the parent shows about 2 levels again; those helpers may nest up to 4, and so on.

```js
// ❌ BAD — 5+ if levels in one function
function labelMap(root) {
  if (root) {
    for (const row of rows) {
      if (kids.length >= 2) {
        if (label) {
          if (label.length <= 80) {
            if (!Object.hasOwn(map, label)) {
              map[label] = value;
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD — parent stays shallow; detail lives in a helper
function labelMap(root) {
  const map = {};

  if (root) {
    for (const row of rows) {
      addLabelFromRow(map, row);
    }
  }

  return map;
}
```
